  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 245 PM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nWe are likely to continue a pattern of quiet weather as next \nweekend approaches. Ensemble guidance places us on the Lee side \nof an upper- level ridge draped across the Rockies. Flow aloft \nis expected to remain from the northwest through Tuesday night, \nbriefly becoming meridional Wednesday, then shifting back to the\nnorthwest for the rest of the four-day forecast period. Said \nridge is anticipated to amplify a bit heading into the weekend \nas well. There is high confidence of a shortwave moving through \nthe County Warning Area (CWA) Tuesday into Wednesday. Guidance \nalso shows that nothing is expected to come from this other than\nwinds aloft shifting briefly to the north. Recent surface \nanalysis does show a stationary front over the CWA Tuesday, but \nthis should exit the area before Wednesday arrives. Rainfall \nshould not be a factor and warmer temperatures stick around \nheading into next Saturday.\n\nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) are likely to be less than \n5% for the entire Tri-State region through Friday of next week. \nDry air aloft plus high pressure is expected to keep any cloud \ndevelopment limited to diurnal cumulus. High temperatures across\nthe region are likely to consistently sit around the low to mid\n80s through Friday. Modest dew points in the 40s/50s for the \nmajority of the CWA are expected with some areas east of Kansas \nHighway 25 seeing up to low 60s. \n\nAs we head into fire weather season, Relative Humidity (RH) and\nsurface winds become even more relevant for the region. For \nthis forecast period in particular, RH values are expected to \ndip during each day Tuesday through Friday as diurnal heating \ntakes place. RH values in the 20s will be likely for the eastern\nColorado counties through Friday with a 60-70% chance to fall \ninto the teens. In NW Kansas and SW Nebraska, 40-60% should be \ncommonplace during the day. Surface winds Tuesday are expected \nto shift from a southerly 5-15 mph to more from the north across\nthe Colorado counties as well as southwest winds elsewhere. \nWeaker 5-10 mph north winds are likely to shift to more of an \neast wind on Wednesday. A west to southeast shift is anticipated\nThursday and Friday's winds should be from the south/southeast,\nboth at 5-15 mph. Gusting up to 20-25 mph is possible during \nthe forecast period. Despite low humidity in eastern Colorado, \nwinds are not expected to be of significant concern. Thus, fire \nweather potential there should remain low for the time being.",
